(5598994, 807411, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hello from the CC Club!!<br/> <br/>1st Impressions, This is a very straight on shot, the composition could have been a little different.  <br/> <br/>You have a leaf in the water there, and if you look at teh water, it is almost shaped like a leaf.  If you have dropped it closer and to the left, i think it would have had a greater impact on the viewer and been a stronger photo. <br/> <br/>More contrast and sharpness would have brought out some of the details in the stone. You have the right idea for the challenge and the I would love to see some more PP work on this. <br/> <br/>Keep on shooting<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hello from the CC Club!! 1st Impressions, This is a very straight on shot, the composition could have been a little different.   You have a leaf in the water there, and if you look at teh water, it is almost shaped like a leaf.  If you have dropped it closer and to the left, i think it would have had a greater impact on the viewer and been a stronger photo.  More contrast and sharpness would have brought out some of the details in the stone. You have the right idea for the challenge and the I would love to see some more PP work on this.  Keep on shooting ', '2009-08-04 22:53:03.000000', NULL, 0),
(5599054, 806522, 87808, '<td valign="top">Hi from the CC Club!<br/> <br/>Looks like it was a cold day there!<br/> <br/>First thing I noticed was your ISO, wow I never knew it could go down to 50.<br/> <br/>It was a brave choice to go black and white with this shot, I think you could have gone with a longer shutter speed on this one for the creamy smooth like waves that is very popular right now.  This would have contrasted nicely with the sharp jagged rocks. <br/> <br/>Your rocks are interesting in their shapes, but I am not sure if it is the conversion to BW that has made the seem very over sharpened, there is a halo around the rocks from it. Playing around with different positions would have eliminated the ship and the branch.  In post processing, if you use the selective colours and hue and saturation, then turn it into a BW, you will find that it brings out some amazing contrasts and details that get overlooked. <br/> <br/>I am looking forward to seeing more of your work!<br/> <br/></td>', 'Hi from the CC Club! Looks like it was a cold day there! First thing I noticed was your ISO, wow I never knew it could go down to 50. It was a brave choice to go black and white with this shot, I think you could have gone with a longer shutter speed on this one for the creamy smooth like waves that is very popular right now.  This would have contrasted nicely with the sharp jagged rocks.  Your rocks are interesting in their shapes, but I am not sure if it is the conversion to BW that has made the seem very over sharpened, there is a halo around the rocks from it. Playing around with different positions would have eliminated the ship and the branch.  In post processing, if you use the selective colours and hue and saturation, then turn it into a BW, you will find that it brings out some amazing contrasts and details that get overlooked.  I am looking forward to seeing more of your work! ', '2009-08-04 23:19:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(5599061, 807372, 69246, '<td valign="top">** Hello from the Critique Club **<br/> <br/>Very interesting image, I like your progression of geology from the rock to the pure elements.<br/> <br/><b>Composition</b> <br/>The main problem with this photo is in the composition: it is too flat. I do like the progression from the larger rock background to the smaller rock, to the smaller elements. This progression is a benefit to this photo. However, every object is essentially in the same plane. A tighter angle with more texture on the rock holding the elements would have led to a more dynamic photo.<br/> <br/><b>Technical</b> <br/>The lighting, while soft, is flat, leading to the compositional comment above. A second source of light would help to bring out the texture in the rock and add depth to the photo. The lighting then effectively places everything in the same plane.<br/> <br/>Overall, it is interesting from the placement of the objects. It would be good to experiment with additional light sources and get in tighter with the shot, perhaps dropping the background out altogether to help isolate the stone itself. It definitely has promise and would be a good one to enter in a Free Study.</td>', '** Hello from the Critique Club ** Very interesting image, I like your progression of geology from the rock to the pure elements. Composition The main problem with this photo is in the composition: it is too flat. I do like the progression from the larger rock background to the smaller rock, to the smaller elements. This progression is a benefit to this photo. However, every object is essentially in the same plane. A tighter angle with more texture on the rock holding the elements would have led to a more dynamic photo. Technical The lighting, while soft, is flat, leading to the compositional comment above. A second source of light would help to bring out the texture in the rock and add depth to the photo. The lighting then effectively places everything in the same plane. Overall, it is interesting from the placement of the objects. It would be good to experiment with additional light sources and get in tighter with the shot, perhaps dropping the background out altogether to help isolate the stone itself. It definitely has promise and would be a good one to enter in a Free Study.', '2009-08-04 23:23:30.000000', NULL, 0),
(5599081, 807154, 87808, '<td valign="top">CC Club here. <br/> <br/>Well, as pointed out, it is pretty much out of focus. It would have been a great shot in focus. The silica in the sand would have popped out and all those colours would have made this a very great shot <br/> <br/></td>', 'CC Club here.  Well, as pointed out, it is pretty much out of focus. It would have been a great shot in focus. The silica in the sand would have popped out and all those colours would have made this a very great shot  ', '2009-08-04 23:35:12.000000', NULL, 0),
